WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.135
Back to the bank's vault: one door, one key on a hook.

2
00:00:06.135 --> 00:00:14.596
At closing time, the interest job, a thread that adds interest to every account, takes the key, and keeps it for a second and a half.

3
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.446
Just then, a customer at a cash machine asks for her balance.

4
00:00:18.446 --> 00:00:22.153
With synchronized, her request joins the line and stays there.

5
00:00:22.153 --> 00:00:24.783
She watches a spinning wheel the whole time.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.806
So here's a question that sounds a little rude.

7
00:00:27.806 --> 00:00:31.507
synchronized works, and it never forgets to give the key back.

8
00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:33.596
Why would anyone want another lock?

9
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.852
Because its line has no way out.

10
00:00:35.852 --> 00:00:39.314
A waiting thread can't give up after 200 milliseconds.

11
00:00:39.314 --> 00:00:45.212
And an interrupt, another thread asking it to stop, doesn't reach it until it holds the key.

12
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.048
Java has one: the same vault, with controls on the door.

13
00:00:50.048 --> 00:00:55.135
Same rules: one thread inside at a time, and the next one sees every change.

14
00:00:55.135 --> 00:00:59.419
The owner may even walk back in, so Java calls it ReentrantLock.

15
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:01.002
Here are the controls.

16
00:01:01.002 --> 00:01:04.114
tryLock takes the key only if it's free right now.

17
00:01:04.114 --> 00:01:07.039
Give it a time, and it waits at most that long.

18
00:01:07.039 --> 00:01:11.457
And lockInterruptibly waits, but leaves the line when it's interrupted.

19
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.359
Let's play out the evening.

20
00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:16.808
The interest job holds the key for a second and a half.

21
00:01:16.808 --> 00:01:20.193
The cash machine waits with tryLock, 200 milliseconds.

22
00:01:20.193 --> 00:01:23.391
The statement printer waits with lockInterruptibly.

23
00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:26.463
And an old teller screen still uses synchronized.

24
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:33.480
400 milliseconds after they arrive, the program starts to shut down, and interrupts the printer and the teller.

25
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.365
Which one keeps waiting after it's interrupted?

26
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:46.188
First, at 200 milliseconds, the cash machine gives up, and asks the customer to try again.

27
00:01:46.188 --> 00:01:48.009
An answer, right on time.

28
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:49.878
Then the interrupt arrives.

29
00:01:49.878 --> 00:01:52.620
The printer leaves the line at once, cleanly.

30
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:54.417
The teller hears nothing.

31
00:01:54.417 --> 00:02:01.956
It waits until the interest job is done, gets in, and only then finds out it was asked to stop, about a second earlier.

32
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:07.601
It's like finding the note that says the meeting is off while you sit alone in the meeting room.

33
00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:10.884
A thread waiting for synchronized can't hear an interrupt.

34
00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:14.425
All that control has a price.

35
00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:18.750
synchronized gives the key back when its block ends, even on an error.

36
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.211
A ReentrantLock gives it back only when you call unlock.

37
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:26.671
So picture a thread that takes the lock and adds an entry to the ledger.

38
00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:29.026
One day a bad input makes the add throw.

39
00:02:29.026 --> 00:02:33.440
The unlock never runs, and the thread walks off with the key in its pocket.

40
00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:36.591
Every thread after it parks in lock, for good.

41
00:02:36.591 --> 00:02:36.909
Well.

42
00:02:36.909 --> 00:02:40.215
That's a long line for a key that left the building.

43
00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:43.227
So every use follows one pattern.

44
00:02:43.227 --> 00:02:44.690
lock, just before try.

45
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.754
The work inside.

46
00:02:45.754 --> 00:02:47.814
unlock, first thing in finally.

47
00:02:47.814 --> 00:02:53.598
Read it aloud: take the key, try the work, and finally, whatever happens, give it back.

48
00:02:53.598 --> 00:02:56.391
Kotlin's withLock writes all that for you.

49
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.749
A ReentrantLock lets go only when you tell it to.

50
00:02:59.749 --> 00:03:04.891
So keep synchronized for short, simple code, and switch when you need a control.

51
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.718
A lock is also a place to wait.

52
00:03:07.718 --> 00:03:09.085
Remember the kitchen?

53
00:03:09.085 --> 00:03:17.027
A monitor has one bench, so waiters waiting for room sat with cooks waiting for a ticket, and we had to ring for everyone.

54
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.773
A ReentrantLock can have many benches.

55
00:03:19.773 --> 00:03:24.589
Each one is a Condition: a line of threads waiting for one kind of change.

56
00:03:24.589 --> 00:03:26.997
Cooks wait on notEmpty, for a ticket.

57
00:03:26.997 --> 00:03:29.209
Waiters wait on notFull, for room.

58
00:03:29.433 --> 00:03:29.838
Watch.

59
00:03:29.838 --> 00:03:32.876
A waiter clips a ticket and signals notEmpty.

60
00:03:32.876 --> 00:03:36.117
Only the cooks' bell rings, so one cook gets up.

61
00:03:36.117 --> 00:03:40.438
The next waiter finds the rail full, and calls await on notFull.

62
00:03:40.438 --> 00:03:43.611
Like wait, it hangs the key back and sits down.

63
00:03:44.433 --> 00:03:45.421
Is it worth it?

64
00:03:45.421 --> 00:03:50.755
Four waiters and four cooks move 20,000 tickets through a rail with room for one.

65
00:03:50.755 --> 00:03:54.311
How often does a thread wake, look, and sit back down?

66
00:03:54.311 --> 00:03:56.023
First one bench, then two.

67
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.824
With one bench, 88,340 times, in about 2.2 seconds.

68
00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:10.533
With two benches, 375 times, in about 0.7 seconds.

69
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:15.395
One bench is like a whole office standing up each time one phone rings.

70
00:04:15.395 --> 00:04:19.698
It isn't zero, because a running thread can grab the ticket first.

71
00:04:19.698 --> 00:04:21.197
So keep the while loop.

72
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.690
Last, the bank's table of exchange rates.

73
00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:31.184
Cash machines read it about 5,000 times a second, and a refresh job replaces it every five seconds.

74
00:04:31.184 --> 00:04:35.055
With a plain lock, readers wait for each other for nothing.

75
00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:37.434
Think of a reading room in a library.

76
00:04:37.434 --> 00:04:39.796
Any number of people can read at once.

77
00:04:39.796 --> 00:04:46.076
To rearrange the shelves, the librarian waits for an empty room, then works alone with the door shut.

78
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.359
That's a ReentrantReadWriteLock.

79
00:04:48.359 --> 00:04:50.160
Readers share the read lock.

80
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.277
The refresh job takes the write lock, which needs an empty room.

81
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.076
Now a trap.

82
00:04:56.076 --> 00:04:58.191
A reader finds a rate missing.

83
00:04:58.191 --> 00:05:02.140
Still holding its read lock, it asks for the write lock.

84
00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.974
No other thread is around.

85
00:05:03.974 --> 00:05:04.891
What happens?

86
00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:08.896
It waits forever.

87
00:05:08.896 --> 00:05:13.499
The write lock waits for every reader to leave, and this thread is a reader.

88
00:05:13.499 --> 00:05:16.830
It's waiting for the room to empty while sitting in it.

89
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.435
Java's deadlock check won't even report it.

90
00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:22.705
That's called an upgrade, and this lock can't do it.

91
00:05:22.705 --> 00:05:29.600
So let go of the read lock, take the write lock, then check again: another thread may have loaded the rate in between.

92
00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.677
So why another lock?

93
00:05:31.677 --> 00:05:34.932
Because sometimes a waiting thread needs a way out.

94
00:05:34.932 --> 00:05:41.443
It's the same vault with controls on the door, a bench for each kind of waiter, and readers can share.

95
00:05:41.443 --> 00:05:44.827
But it never lets go by itself, so unlock in finally.

96
00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:45.906
Quick check.

97
00:05:45.906 --> 00:05:49.540
A cash machine must answer within 50 milliseconds.

98
00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:54.120
Which fits: lockInterruptibly, or tryLock with 50 milliseconds?
